<p>President Trump is publicly pushing back on reports that the Pentagon’s top officer, Joint Chiefs chair General Dan Caine, warned him about the risks of striking Iran. Trump called the reporting “100 percent incorrect”. Even so, he has said he would rather reach a deal with Tehran than go to war—while still warning there will be consequences if negotiations fail. The administration is preparing for another round of indirect talks with Iran in Geneva on Thursday, and Iran is expected to send a proposal beforehand. Multiple outlets also point to a sizable U.S. military build-up meant to pressure Tehran, alongside real uncertainty about Washington’s end goal—and whether pressure will produce concessions or push both sides toward escalation. Meanwhile, the State Department ordered non-emergency staff to leave the U.S. Embassy in Beirut after a security review, while the embassy remains open with core staff. Inside Iran, student-led anti-government protests have continued for a third day at several universities, according to witnesses and verified videos—adding domestic pressure as regional tensions rise.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/c8X_WFHaN4mdDTjjAbBD2_i4EtbIIll40o7wdtXawWtqJSIrQ25O6IP-PsvtoCUA87tjo_sekHK1cN55PoRZc_Pw_Cazv73g7PMJxEyvT4a0e1p4cFyliV6tdcX5SSTi1qOobFrF2yrQptl6YZCeTBrqIoGkIprq0qHnrS4cJveSwYiRsdlu4OOj-DLnAmMWyMnSdnjCart-ZYfpf5xZL7G9vtT-9tteyg-VaebXNjpZSye1UEN5t74tB2nCAeBtFRJqca3yFLj3IPPgcA' alt='A worker paints decorations ahead of Ramadan in downtown Beirut, Lebanon.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Envoy influence: The Guardian reports that Trump’s thinking on possible airstrikes is being shaped in part by special envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner, who are focused on whether Iran is stalling over a nuclear deal.</li><li>Iran warning: Iran has warned it would react “ferociously” to any U.S. attack, saying a strike could spark a wider regional conflict.</li><li>Aim ambiguity: Analyses say the scale of the U.S. military build-up is meant to increase pressure on Tehran, but Washington’s exact end goal remains unclear—raising the risk that coercion dynamics could fuel escalation instead of concessions.</li><li>Campus unrest details: Beyond protests at Tehran University and al-Zahra University, reports describe scuffles at Amir Kabir University and episodes such as flag-burning and anti-government chants—though the unrest has largely stayed on campuses.</li></ul><blockquote>Everything that has been written about a potential War with Iran has been written incorrectly, and purposefully so. - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: He says he prefers a negotiated deal but insists reporting about internal military warnings and war planning has been wrong, while warning Tehran of consequences if talks fail. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/trump-said-iran-strike-would-be-easily-won-but-prefers-deal'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Iranian government: Iran warns it will retaliate strongly to any U.S. attack and cautions that escalation could spread into a broader regional conflict. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/iran-would-react-ferociously-any-us-attack-warns-regional-conflict-0'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>U.S. State Department: After a security review, it ordered non-essential Beirut embassy personnel to depart while keeping the embassy open with core staff. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cj32l00k47lo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: It argues the U.S. military build-up was designed to coerce Tehran but has not achieved that goal, raising the risk of a crisis driven by escalation dynamics. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/0408deca-ecca-49ab-bff6-985ef83897ce'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Iranian student protesters: Students have staged campus demonstrations and anti-government chants for several days, as shown in verified videos and witness accounts, despite recent crackdowns. (<a hr